WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.939
For a long time, working from home was reserved for a few professions: translators, writers, freelance graphic designers. The 2020 health crisis turned everything upside down. Overnight, millions of employees turned their kitchens into offices, and many never fully went back to the workplace.

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:41.758
The advantages are well known. Less time lost commuting means more time to sleep, exercise or look after one's children. Some employees even claim to be more efficient at home, far from the constant interruptions of the open-plan office. For companies, remote work makes it possible to reduce office space and to attract candidates who live far away.

00:00:42.008 --> 00:01:09.079
However, the picture is not entirely rosy. Without colleagues around, the line between work and private life becomes blurred: you check your email in the evening, you answer a message on Sunday. Young employees, in particular, risk suffering from this. It is often by watching others, by asking a question at the coffee machine, that one learns a trade. Yet these informal exchanges come across badly through a screen.

00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:29.307
Remote work also deepens less visible inequalities. It requires a home big enough to set up a desk, a reliable connection and a certain personal discipline. Yet not every employee has these, and many jobs — cashier, nurse, factory worker — simply cannot be done remotely.

00:01:29.557 --> 00:01:47.501
Most companies now seem to be moving towards a hybrid solution: two or three days at the office, the rest at home. This compromise doesn't satisfy everyone, but it has the merit of recognising a reality: we don't work only to produce, but also to belong to a group.
